  "articleBody": "Planning garage drywall installation for an attached garage involves more than just hanging gypsum board. Because your garage shares walls or a ceiling with living spaces, local residential codes generally require a specific fire-separation assembly on the garage side. Standard half-inch drywall often does not meet requirements when sleeping rooms or living areas sit directly overhead, where 5/8-inch Type X board is frequently specified. Choosing the right crew means picking a contractor who checks framing, plans for insulation, and seals joints properly rather than treating the garage like an ordinary shed.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nThe layout of your home determines the materials and labor required. An attached garage with habitable living space above requires a protective ceiling assembly, whereas a detached workshop may allow standard panels. Any plumbing, ductwork, or electrical penetrations crossing the separation wall require approved firestopping to maintain protection. Overlooking these details can cause failed building inspections and safety hazards.\n\nWhen you want clean finish work and dependable installation across the Denver area, HH Remodeling LLC provides hands-on craftsmanship led by owner Abraham Becerra. Call 303-505-0866 or email Hhremodelingllc1986.ab@gmail.com to discuss your garage project.",
